# Line endings.
# ============================================================================
# The last matching line wins, so the catch-all goes first and the exceptions
# after it. Written the other way round the exceptions are silently overridden,
# which is what happened here: the two rules below said "verbatim" while
# `git check-attr` said `text: auto`.

# Source is normalised on the way in, so a checkout on Windows and one on Linux
# hold the same bytes.
* text=auto
*.sh text eol=lf

# Data this project reads, not source it edits. Carried byte for byte.
#
# The SolidWorks libraries because the parsers are written against the bytes
# SolidWorks writes — CRLF and all — and because one of them is checked against
# a published copy, which only means anything if it is unaltered.
*.p2m   -text
*.sldmat -text

# The CAD samples because the tests read them at fixed byte offsets: truncating
# at 1024 bytes and flipping the byte at 700 say nothing about a file whose
# every newline grew by one on the way out of the repository.
*.x_t     -text
*.xmt_txt -text
*.stp     -text
*.step    -text
*.p21     -text

# Binary, and never worth a diff.
*.glb  binary
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
